- pastream-reopen-after-close-segfault: opening a PaStream (either constructor form), closing
  it, then opening a second PaStream in the same process segfaults inside libportaudio's ALSA
  host API - reproduces with the plain positional constructor alone (`Pa_OpenDefaultStream`
  twice, closing in between), so it is not specific to the new object-form constructor or to
  this binding's C code. Confirmed present on the unmodified `quickjs-portaudio.c` too (tested
  by stashing the doc/portaudio.md implementation changes and rebuilding). Likely an
  ALSA/PortAudio-level issue specific to this sandboxed environment (no real audio hardware,
  JACK not running, only dummy/pulse ALSA devices - see the ALSA/JACK connection errors printed
  on every Pa_Initialize()), but not yet root-caused past that.

    import * as pa from 'portaudio';
    pa.Pa_Initialize();
    new pa.PaStream(0, 2).close();
    new pa.PaStream(0, 2); // segfaults during Pa_OpenDefaultStream

- pastream-getters-no-closed-guard (quickjs-portaudio.c: js_pastream_get): unlike the methods
  (read/write/start/stop/abort/close, which now throw "PaStream is closed"), the property
  getters (active, stopped, time, cpuLoad, readAvailable, writeAvailable) call their
  PortAudio function directly on `st` with no NULL check after `.close()` has set
  `w->stream = NULL`. Several of these don't crash on a NULL PaStream* but silently return a
  wrong value instead - e.g. `.stopped` reads back `true` after close only because
  `Pa_IsStreamStopped(NULL)`'s negative PaError return happens to coerce truthy through
  `JS_NewBool()`, not because the query is meaningful. `inputLatency`/`outputLatency`/
  `sampleRate` are safe (guarded by `Pa_GetStreamInfo(st)` already returning NULL), but the
  rest are not.

    let s = new portaudio.PaStream(0, 2); s.close(); s.stopped; // true, not because it's meaningfully "stopped"

- devices-hostapis-interleave-segfault: a specific call sequence involving no PaStream at all
  reliably segfaults - iterate every `devices[i]` (or just access enough of them), then call
  `new PaDeviceInfo(index)`, then iterate `hostApis[i]` reading `.typeName`, then access
  `devices[...]` again afterward. Any one of these steps alone, or most pairs, are fine; only
  this specific interleaving crashes. Root cause not identified (does not reproduce with a
  plain devices-loop + hostApis-loop with no constructor call in between, and does not
  reproduce if the `PaDeviceInfo`/`HostApiInfo` constructor calls are moved to run last, after
  both collections are otherwise fully read - see the workaround note above
  `testDeviceInfoAndHostApiInfoConstructors()` in portaudio-test.js). Likely the same general
  class of environment/heap issue as pastream-reopen-after-close-segfault above, but confirmed
  as a distinct trigger (no stream ever opened here).

    import * as pa from 'portaudio';
    pa.Pa_Initialize();
    for(let i = 0; i < pa.devices.length; i++) pa.devices[i];
    new pa.PaDeviceInfo(pa.devices.defaultOutput);
    for(let i = 0; i < pa.hostApis.length; i++) pa.hostApis[i].typeName;
    pa.devices[pa.devices.defaultOutput].hostApi; // segfaults here
